L["prodigal-decides"] = """Tax collectors and sinners were gathering around to hear Jesus. The Pharisees and the teachers of the law muttered, this man welcomes sinners and eats with them. So Jesus told them three stories about lost things, a lost sheep, a lost coin, and a lost son. The third is the best known.

There was a man who had two sons. The younger one said to his father, father, give me my share of the estate. In that culture, asking for an inheritance while your father was still alive was a deep insult. It was like saying, I wish you were dead. Yet the father divided his property between them.

Not long after that, the younger son got together all he had, set off for a distant country, and there squandered his wealth in wild living. He had plenty of friends while the money lasted. After he had spent everything, there was a severe famine in that whole country, and he began to be in need. So he went and hired himself out to a citizen of that country, who sent him to his fields to feed pigs. For a Jewish young man, this was as low as it could get. He longed to fill his stomach with the pods that the pigs were eating, but no one gave him anything.

Then Jesus said, when he came to his senses. The Bible's words are simple and powerful. He was finally seeing the truth. How many of my father's hired servants have food to spare, and here I am starving to death! I will set out and go back to my father and say to him, father, I have sinned against heaven and against you. I am no longer worthy to be called your son. Make me like one of your hired servants. So he got up and went to his father.

Why does this matter? Jesus told this story to people who thought God only welcomed the respectable. It describes a man who made a terrible choice and hit the bottom. But it also shows the turning point, which is honest recognition. He didn't blame the famine or his friends. He said, I have sinned. He rehearsed his speech and he started walking.

What happened after? The next part of the story describes the father who ran to meet him. The son had prepared to ask for a servant's job, but the father had something else in mind. That part is the heart of the parable.

What does this mean for us today? Many people feel that they have gone too far to come back. Jesus told this story to say that the way home is open. It begins with coming to our senses, which means seeing where we really are and what we really need. It doesn't require us to fix ourselves first. The son was still dirty and hungry when he decided to go. And it takes a step. He got up. The turn from far away begins with one decision to head home.

That is why this story is so important. It says that no one is beyond coming home, and that repentance is as simple as turning around. And it prepares the way for the best news in the story, the father who is already watching the road."""

L["prodigal-return"] = """The younger son had rehearsed his speech all the way home. He was still a long way off when something happened that he didn't plan for. His father saw him. The father had been watching the road.

Jesus says that the father was filled with compassion for his son. He ran to his son, threw his arms around him and kissed him. In that culture, an older man did not run. It was undignified. But this father didn't care what anyone thought. He ran, and in that moment he took on the shame that his son deserved.

The son began his speech. Father, I have sinned against heaven and against you. I am no longer worthy to be called your son. But he didn't get to finish the last part, the part about being made like a hired servant. His father cut him off. The father said to his servants, quick! Bring the best robe and put it on him. Put a ring on his finger and sandals on his feet. Bring the fattened calf and kill it. Let's have a feast and celebrate. For this son of mine was dead and is alive again. He was lost and is found. So they began to celebrate.

The robe, the ring and the sandals were not small gifts. The robe covered his rags, the ring carried the family authority, and sandals were worn by sons, not servants. The father didn't restore him as an employee. He restored him as a son.

Meanwhile, the older son was in the field. When he came near the house, he heard music and dancing. He called one of the servants and asked what was going on. Your brother has come, he replied, and your father has killed the fattened calf because he has him back safe and sound. The older brother became angry and refused to go in. So his father went out and pleaded with him.

He answered his father, look! All these years I've been slaving for you and never disobeyed your orders. Yet you never gave me even a young goat so I could celebrate with my friends. But when this son of yours who has squandered your property with prostitutes comes home, you kill the fattened calf for him! Notice that he said, this son of yours. He wouldn't call him brother.

My son, the father said, you are always with me, and everything I have is yours. But we had to celebrate and be glad, because this brother of yours was dead and is alive again. He was lost and is found. Jesus ended the story there, without telling us whether the older brother went in.

Why does this matter? The two sons stand for two kinds of people. One ran away with his sins on the surface. The other stayed home and was just as far from the father's heart. The Pharisees listening to Jesus were the older brother. The father loved both and went out to meet both.

What happened after? We don't know whether the older brother joined the party. The story is left open on purpose. Jesus was asking his listeners to decide.

What does this mean for us today? If you have run, the father is watching the road for you. If you have stayed and kept the rules, check whether your heart is still at home. Both sons needed grace. Both could be welcomed.

That is why this story is so important. It shows the heart of God as a Father who runs to meet us. And it points to Jesus, who told it to show who he came to welcome."""
